<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Odysseus</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ulysses</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Odysseus, called Ulysses by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, is the legendary king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ithaca">Ithaca</a> in Greek myth, a hero of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trojan-war">Trojan War</a> famous for his cunning, credited with the stratagem of the wooden horse. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odyssey">Odyssey</a> tells of his ten-year voyage home, including his escape from the Cyclops Polyphemus. Many Greek and Italian cities claimed him or his sons as founders.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/odysseus.jpg" alt="Odysseus" width="320" height="378" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jastrow · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Head_Odysseus_MAR_Sperlonga.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The etymology of the name Odysseus is unknown, and a non-Greek or Pre-Greek origin has been suggested. In Book 19 of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odyssey">Odyssey</a> his grandfather Autolycus names the child, saying that he has been angered with many, men and women, so the boy shall be called Odysseus; the name is thus tied to the verb odussomai, meaning to be wroth against. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a> and the Odyssey his father is Laertes and his mother Anticlea. A non-Homeric tradition made Sisyphus his true father. His wife is Penelope and his son Telemachus.</p><p>Most sources for his exploits before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trojan-war">Trojan War</a>, chiefly the mythographers Pseudo-Apollodorus and Hyginus, postdate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a> by many centuries. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helen-of-troy">Helen of Troy</a> was abducted, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelaus">Menelaus</a> called on the suitors to honour their oaths. Odysseus tried to avoid going, since an oracle had foretold a long-delayed return home if he went, and feigned madness by yoking a donkey and an ox to his plough. Palamedes, at the behest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agamemnon">Agamemnon</a>, placed the infant Telemachus in front of the plough. Odysseus turned it aside, exposing the ruse.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In the Iliad Odysseus is one of the most influential Greek champions and, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nestor">Nestor</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idomeneus">Idomeneus</a>, one of the most trusted counsellors. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agamemnon">Agamemnon</a> tested the army's morale by announcing his intention to depart, Odysseus restored order, and he later persuaded Agamemnon not to withdraw. He was one of the three envoys sent in the failed embassy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achilles">Achilles</a>, and he helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diomedes">Diomedes</a> in the night raid in which Rhesus was killed. After Patroclus was slain he counselled Achilles to let the men eat and rest before returning to battle.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odyssey">Odyssey</a> tells of the ten years of travel that followed the war. Leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a> with twelve ships, he first raided Ismarus, a city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicones">Cicones</a>, allies of the Trojans. The raiders took goods, livestock and women, sparing only Maron, a priest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a>, and his family, who gave Odysseus a barrel of wine. The Cicones returned with help from their neighbours, and after losing six of his men Odysseus was forced to flee.</p><p>Later sources, mostly genealogical, gave him many further children in order to link him with the foundation of Italic cities. Hellanicus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesbos">Lesbos</a> wrote that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> was founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeneas">Aeneas</a> and Odysseus together. Evidence points to a cult of Odysseus on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ithaca">Ithaca</a>, with public games called the Odysseia and a gathering place, the Odysseion. Strabo records an altar to him on the island of Meninx, modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djerba">Djerba</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Odysseus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Iliad and the Odyssey of Homer, composed around the 8th or 7th century BCE, show the gods taking part in the Trojan War and the wanderings of <b>Odysseus</b>, intervening in human affairs, favouring their heroes and quarrelling on Olympus.</blockquote><a href="/religion/ancient-greek-religion">Ancient Greek religion · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>…combined Greek, Etruscan, Latin and Roman elements: Greek vases of the sixth century BCE already show Aeneas founding a new home in Etruria, he was worshipped at Lavinium, and a fragment usually attributed to Hellanicus of Lesbos made him the founder of Rome with the help of <b>Odysseus</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/aeneid">Aeneid · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Its stock epithets and repeated phrases, such as “resourceful <b>Odysseus</b>”, are formulae easily fitted to the hexameter line.</blockquote><a href="/work/iliad">Iliad · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Penelope tests the returned <b>Odysseus</b> by asking to have their bed moved; only he knows it cannot be, because he carved it from the trunk of a living olive tree.</blockquote><a href="/work/odyssey">Odyssey · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>James Joyce parodied it in Finnegans Wake and quoted it in <b>Ulysses</b>, and William Brough parodied the words in his burlesque Lalla Rookh (1857).</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-minstrel-boy">The Minstrel Boy</a></li><li><blockquote>Its name recalled Maron, the priest of Apollo who gave <b>Odysseus</b> the potent wine he used to intoxicate the Cyclops.</blockquote><a href="/state/maroneia">Maroneia</a></li></ul></section>
